---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import OnThisPage from '../components/OnThisPage.astro';
import DayStop from '../components/DayStop.astro';
import Kiosk from '../components/Kiosk.astro';
import TriageBoard from '../components/TriageBoard.astro';
import DeviceRace from '../components/DeviceRace.astro';
import DoctorFlow from '../components/DoctorFlow.astro';
import FloorClock from '../components/FloorClock.astro';
import ClinicMap from '../components/ClinicMap.astro';
import NightlyReport from '../components/NightlyReport.astro';
import Changed from '../components/Changed.astro';
import HowIWork from '../components/HowIWork.astro';
import BeforeThis from '../components/BeforeThis.astro';
import Questions from '../components/Questions.astro';
import Contact from '../components/Contact.astro';
---
<Base>
  <Hero />
  <OnThisPage />

  <!-- The spine: one clinic day, seven parts, each with a recreation of the real tool. -->
  <div id="day" style="scroll-margin-top: var(--bar-h)">

    <DayStop id="checkin" n={1} time="7:55 am · front desk" title="Patients check themselves in. The chart updates itself." next={{ id: 'boards', label: 'Live boards for every role' }}>
      <Fragment slot="text">
        <p class="body">
          The patient drops their health card or driver's licence into a reader. In about a second the kiosk
          finds today's appointment, greets them by name, and asks one question: is your address and phone
          still correct? One tap, and they are marked <b>Here</b> in the EMR. If the card is newer than the chart,
          the chart is corrected on the spot.
        </p>
        <p class="body">No line at the desk. No one re-typing an address. Elderly patients manage it unaided because there is nothing to type.</p>
        <div class="fact"><b>One tap for the patient. Zero for the front desk.</b><span>Live at the front desk since 23 September 2026. A touch-only path is always there for patients without a card.</span></div>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual">
        <Kiosk />
        <p class="screen-cap">The real kiosk's wording, step by step. It steps through on its own, or use the buttons.</p>
      </Fragment>
    </DayStop>

    <DayStop id="boards" n={2} time="8:10 am · every TV in the clinic" title="Every screen shows who is next. Nobody walks the hall to ask." flip next={{ id: 'devices', label: 'Device results into the chart' }}>
      <Fragment slot="text">
        <p class="body">
          Each role gets its own board, read straight from the EMR's daysheet about every ten seconds. The
          doctors' lounge sees who is waiting for each doctor and for how long. The imaging tech sees who is
          ready for a scan and which room is free. The front desk sees who has arrived and who is late.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          When a tech drags a patient into a room or taps <b>Done</b>, the board writes the new status back to
          the EMR. One action, every screen updates, and the day's timeline writes itself.
        </p>
        <div class="fact"><b>One board per role, all reading the same daysheet.</b><span>Doctors' lounge, imaging, visual fields, vision rooms and the front desk. Doctors can open theirs on a phone.</span></div>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual">
        <TriageBoard />
        <ul class="tb-roles" aria-label="who sees what">
          <li><i style="background:var(--s-purple)"></i><span><b>Doctors' lounge:</b> who is waiting for each doctor, longest first.</span></li>
          <li><i style="background:var(--s-yellow)"></i><span><b>Imaging:</b> who is ready for a scan, which room is free.</span></li>
          <li><i style="background:var(--s-orange)"></i><span><b>Visual fields and vision rooms:</b> the same, for those stations.</span></li>
          <li><i style="background:var(--s-green)"></i><span><b>Front desk:</b> who checked in at the kiosk, who needs a hand.</span></li>
        </ul>
      </Fragment>
    </DayStop>

    <DayStop id="devices" n={3} time="9:30 am · imaging room" title="Results reach the chart before the patient reaches the next room." next={{ id: 'doctor', label: 'One click for the doctor' }}>
      <Fragment slot="text">
        <p class="body">
          Diagnostic machines print a slip or save a file, and then someone types the numbers into the chart.
          Eight fields, every patient, every day, with the occasional slipped digit.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          Instead, every device in the clinic exports to one shared folder, and a small script watches it.
          A new file appears, the script reads the values, finds the patient's appointment, and writes the
          measurements into their chart. The tech never touches a keyboard.
        </p>
        <div class="fact"><b>About 200 times faster than typing, with no typos.</b><span>Running since May 2026 on the clinic's biometers. Roughly a dozen devices export to the same shared folder.</span></div>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual">
        <DeviceRace />
      </Fragment>
    </DayStop>

    <DayStop id="doctor" n={4} time="10:40 am · exam lane" title="One click, and the doctor's whole workspace is open." flip next={{ id: 'staff', label: 'Staff schedule and time clock' }}>
      <Fragment slot="text">
        <p class="body">
          Before the exam can start, the doctor needs the chart, the OCT scans, and the retinal photos. Three
          systems, three name searches, two monitors, every patient.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          Now the doctor clicks the patient's name on the lounge board. The chart opens on the first monitor.
          The imaging system and the photo viewer open side by side on the second, already on the right patient
          and the right scan for that visit. Name and date of birth are matched in all three before anything is shown.
        </p>
        <div class="fact"><b>Three systems open in 2 to 13 seconds, down from about 33.</b><span>Timed on the clinic's exam-room PCs, August 2026. A wrong-patient match stops and warns instead of opening.</span></div>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual">
        <DoctorFlow />
      </Fragment>
    </DayStop>

    <DayStop id="staff" n={5} time="12:00 pm · staff room" title="The manager's week runs from one screen." next={{ id: 'map', label: 'The clinic map' }}>
      <Fragment slot="text">
        <p class="body">
          The paper station sheet became a board on the staff-room TV: who is at which station this morning
          and this afternoon, who went home early, and the day's announcement. The manager changes it from
          the office and every screen follows.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          The punch clock went too. Staff check in and out with a PIN on the same board. The manager gets a
          short email at 9:00 and 5:00. Every second Sunday the pay-period timesheet is emailed as a PDF, totals done.
        </p>
        <div class="fact"><b>Punch clock and paper sheet retired. Timesheets send themselves.</b><span>Live since 29 September 2026. Imported the old clock's history so nothing was lost in the switch.</span></div>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual">
        <FloorClock />
        <p class="screen-cap">The floor board with the time clock built in. Staff names are fictional.</p>
      </Fragment>
    </DayStop>

    <DayStop id="map" n={6} time="3:15 pm · anywhere in the clinic" title="Staff can watch the day move across the floor." flip next={{ id: 'nightly', label: 'The nightly report' }}>
      <Fragment slot="text">
        <p class="body">
          The same status changes that drive the boards also drive a pixel-art map of the clinic. Patients
          appear at the desk, walk to the field machine, to imaging, into an exam lane, and out.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          It is the one screen in the clinic that is just for fun, and it is also the clearest picture of a
          day anyone there has had. Play it below: walk one visit and watch the boards update as you go.
        </p>
        <div class="fact"><b>Every step you walk is also a row of data.</b><span>Door-to-door time, with no typing, is what the nightly report is built from.</span></div>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual">
        <ClinicMap />
        <p class="screen-cap">Built with the clinic's real floor plan. Fictional patients. Best on a laptop or desktop.</p>
      </Fragment>
    </DayStop>

    <DayStop id="nightly" n={7} time="11:00 pm · after close" title="At night, the clinic reviews its own day." next={{ id: 'changed', label: 'What changed, with numbers' }}>
      <Fragment slot="text">
        <p class="body">
          Every status change on every board is logged with a time, automatically. After close, a model reads
          the day: how long each patient waited at each step, which rooms sat empty while people waited, and
          which choices moved the day along.
        </p>
        <p class="body">
          The result is a short written report, in plain language, before the first patient arrives. Over
          weeks it learns the clinic's own patterns, and on the boards it can quietly suggest who to take
          next and why. The staff always decide.
        </p>
        <div class="fact"><b>The first model learned from 5,941 real staffing decisions.</b><span>It picks the same next patient as experienced staff 51% of the time on the first try; the best simple rule manages 24%. Age is never used to order patients.</span></div>
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="visual">
        <NightlyReport />
        <p class="screen-cap">An illustrative night's report, in the shape of the real one. The numbers change every day.</p>
      </Fragment>
    </DayStop>
  </div>

  <Changed />
  <HowIWork />
  <BeforeThis />
  <Questions />
  <Contact />
</Base>
